        &lt;kb_cause&gt;&lt;![CDATA[&lt;p&gt;This may occur because of a timing issue that prevents the computer from detecting the USB device.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;This has been investigated by operating system and hardware vendors but the results were inconclusive as this is intermittent.&lt;/p&gt;]]&gt;&lt;/kb_cause&gt;
        &lt;kb_description&gt;If you quickly and repeatedly insert and remove a USB device, the port may stop responding&lt;/kb_description&gt;
        &lt;kb_knowledge_base display_value="Known Error"&gt;c0a54bac871023000e3dd61e36cb0bcb&lt;/kb_knowledge_base&gt;
        &lt;kb_workaround&gt;&lt;![CDATA[&lt;p&gt;The USB port might not be broken. Try one of these workarounds:&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Workaround 1: Use Device Manager to scan for hardware changes&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;You will need administrative credentials to perform some of the workarounds.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;Use the Device Manager to scan for hardware changes. Your computer may then recognize the USB device that is connected to the USB port.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;ol style="list-style-position: inside;"&gt;&lt;li&gt;Click &lt;strong&gt;Start&lt;/strong&gt;, and then &lt;strong&gt;Run&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;Type devmgmt.msc into the search box and select Ok. Device Manager opens.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;In Device Manager, select your computer.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;Click Action and then Scan for hardware changes.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;Check to see if the USB device is now working.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ol&gt; &lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Workaround 2: Restart your PC&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;If scanning for hardware changes didn’t work. Try restarting your computer, then check the USB device to see if it now works.&lt;/p&gt;]]&gt;&lt;/kb_workaround&gt;
